      "id": 1,
      "text": "Coldwater was named after Coldwater, Michigan, when settlers preempted land and platted a town site in 1884.",
      "sourceSentenceIds": [
        1
      ],
      "sourceLabels": [
        "Wikipedia"
      ],
      "source": "https://en.wikipedia.org/wiki/Coldwater%2C_Kansas"
    },
    {
      "id": 2,
      "text": "The town became the county seat of Comanche County and still houses the historic Comanche County Historical Museum on Main Street.",
      "sourceSentenceIds": [
        3,
        8
      ],
      "sourceLabels": [
        "Wikipedia",
        "Web source 2"
      ],
      "source": "https://en.wikipedia.org/wiki/Coldwater%2C_Kansas"
    },
    {
      "id": 3,
      "text": "The first post office in Coldwater opened on October 17, 1884, marking the town’s early communication hub.",
      "sourceSentenceIds": [
        15
      ],
      "sourceLabels": [
        "Wikipedia"
      ],
      "source": "https://en.wikipedia.org/wiki/Coldwater%2C_Kansas"
    },
    {
      "id": 4,
      "text": "Coldwater’s climate features hot, humid summers and generally mild to cool winters, typical of western Kansas.",
      "sourceSentenceIds": [
        16
      ],
      "sourceLabels": [
        "Wikipedia"
      ],
      "source": "https://en.wikipedia.org/wiki/Coldwater%2C_Kansas"
    },
    {
      "id": 5,
      "text": "The historic Chief Theater, listed on the National Register of Historic Places, sits at 122 East Main Street.",
      "sourceSentenceIds": [
